// Module `int`, extended (LANG-7): library members of the core module `int` (SEM-1). The
// core members (wrapping_, checked_, saturating_, widen, narrow, try_narrow, truncate,
// parse) are declared in core/core/int.hirsel, generic over the integer types.
//
// A library function cannot be generic over the integer types: no bound names them
// (OWN-4), so a library `[T]` admits every type and its body cannot do arithmetic on T.
// Radix parsing is therefore written once at u64 and once at i64, as Go's ParseUint and
// ParseInt; a narrower result is `int.try_narrow` of it. Nothing here allocates.

/// Parses the whole of s as an unsigned number in `base` (2 to 36), digits as char.digit
/// reads them; no sign, prefix, spaces or '_'.
/// Fails: Bounds (failure) when base is outside 2..=36; Error(Empty | Invalid(i) |
/// OutOfRange), i the byte position. Cost: O(len).
/// Ex: `let cp: Option[u32] = int.try_narrow(int.parse_uint(hex4, 16)?)`
pub fn parse_uint(s: String, base: u32) -> Result[u64, ParseError]

/// As parse_uint, with an optional leading '-'. Ex: `let d = int.parse_int(field, 10)?`
pub fn parse_int(s: String, base: u32) -> Result[i64, ParseError]
